<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/expert/geophysics-of-plate-driving-forces">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Plate-driving forces are <strong>the remaining hard problem of plate tectonics</strong>: the
    theory is 60 years old and confirmed, but the exact mechanics — how the heat engine converts
    internal energy into moving plates — still generates papers, neutrino measurements and
    tomographic imaging campaigns.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the power budget, the convection reality, the force balance, open problems" widget="equationStep" config={driverCfg} />

  <h2>How geophysicists know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Seismic tomography = planetary CT:</strong> earthquake waves image slabs (cold, fast) and plumes (hot, slow) through the whole mantle — the D" layer at the core–mantle boundary hosts two giant slow provinces under Africa and the Pacific whose role is active research.</li>
    <li><strong>GPS measures plates directly:</strong> plate motions measured to millimetres per year match the million-year geological record — two independent clocks agreeing is the strongest confirmation (the remote-sensing lesson\'s precision on land).</li>
    <li><strong>Numerical mantle modelling:</strong> codes like StagYY and ASPECT simulate billion-year convection — reproducing supercontinent cycles (Pangaea was the latest; amalgamation every ~500 Myr) and testing which parameter combinations match Earth.</li>
    <li><strong>Why tectonics matters beyond earthquakes:</strong> the carbon-silicate cycle (weathering + volcanism) regulates climate over geological time — plate tectonics may be a habitability ingredient (the planetary lesson asks the comparative question).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Forsyth & Uyeda (1975) force analysis; KamLAND geoneutrino results (2005); van der Hilst tomography reviews; Tackley computational geodynamics.</p>
</TopicPage>
